1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,603
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,623 --> 00:00:07,740
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:14,858
This is Lecture 17, control of computerised flat knitting machines,

4
00:00:14,878 --> 00:00:18,076
the machines that knit sweaters.

5
00:00:18,476 --> 00:00:24,879
On every sewing machine so far the needle works in one place while the fabric moves.

6
00:00:24,899 --> 00:00:29,390
A flat machine turns that around. The needle beds stay put,

7
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:31,684
the carriage sweeps over them,

8
00:00:31,704 --> 00:00:37,037
and on every pass each of hundreds of needles decides whether to knit.

9
00:00:37,437 --> 00:00:40,870
Three questions today. How is a needle selected?

10
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:44,252
Why must the command go out early, by position?

11
00:00:44,272 --> 00:00:49,073
And how long does one front panel take? The FPGA selection channel,

12
00:00:49,093 --> 00:00:54,398
patterns and closed-loop control of loop length are left to the slides and

13
00:00:54,418 --> 00:00:55,477
the assignment.

14
00:00:56,997 --> 00:00:58,217
Here's the problem.

15
00:00:58,617 --> 00:01:08,755
A knitwear floor is making jacquard sweater fronts on an E12 single-system

16
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:11,776
flat knitting machine:

17
00:01:11,795 --> 00:01:16,168
275 needles, 760 courses in all.

18
00:01:16,568 --> 00:01:23,281
The carriage tops out at 1.2 metres per second with 10 metres per second squared,

19
00:01:23,301 --> 00:01:27,188
and the controller uses a fixed selection lead.

20
00:01:27,588 --> 00:01:30,824
Engineering has three proposals.

21
00:01:31,224 --> 00:01:36,364
A: compensate by real-time speed and shorten the reversal margin.

22
00:01:36,764 --> 00:01:44,400
B: a two-system carriage that knits both colours in one pass.

23
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:50,220
C: raise carriage speed to 1.6 metres per second.

24
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:55,800
How long does a panel take now, and which option is worth it?

25
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,180
We'll answer at the end.

26
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:04,080
Needle beds, latch needles and cams.

27
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,078
First the structure.

28
00:02:07,478 --> 00:02:14,155
A flat machine has a front and a back needle bed forming an inverted V,

29
00:02:14,175 --> 00:02:16,418
about 100 degrees apart.

30
00:02:16,818 --> 00:02:23,306
Each bed has hundreds of slots with a latch needle in each.

31
00:02:23,706 --> 00:02:28,061
The gap at the top is the trick plate gap where loops form,

32
00:02:28,081 --> 00:02:33,326
and the knitted fabric hangs down from it, pulled by take-down rollers.

33
00:02:33,726 --> 00:02:39,742
The carriage rides over both beds; its cams push the needles up and down,

34
00:02:39,762 --> 00:02:45,117
and the yarn carriers travel with it, laying yarn into the hooks.

35
00:02:45,517 --> 00:02:51,015
How dense the needles are is the gauge E, needles per inch,

36
00:02:51,035 --> 00:02:55,036
and the pitch p is 25.4 millimetres over E.

37
00:02:56,556 --> 00:03:03,416
Unroll the carriage's cam plate onto a flat sheet and you get this picture.

38
00:03:03,816 --> 00:03:07,042
The cams are just the cams of Lecture 6,

39
00:03:07,062 --> 00:03:10,612
except the feed cam rotates and these slide,

40
00:03:10,632 --> 00:03:15,561
and the followers are hundreds of needles, one after another,

41
00:03:15,581 --> 00:03:17,914
all tracing the same profile.

42
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:21,315
How do different needles take different paths?

43
00:03:21,715 --> 00:03:22,270
Selection.

44
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:27,143
Two selection points work together: selected at S1 and S2,

45
00:03:27,163 --> 00:03:32,333
the needle rises to clearing height and knits; selected only at S1,

46
00:03:32,353 --> 00:03:36,825
it rises to tuck height; not selected at S1, it stays put.

47
00:03:37,225 --> 00:03:42,655
The electronics makes just one or two yes-or-no decisions per needle;

48
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:47,315
the hard part is making them at exactly the right position.

49
00:03:48,835 --> 00:03:50,291
Selecting by position.

50
00:03:51,811 --> 00:04:01,911
As the carriage sweeps at speed v, the time between two needles is p over v.

51
00:04:02,311 --> 00:04:08,646
At E12 and 1.2 metres per second that's only 1.76 milliseconds,

52
00:04:08,666 --> 00:04:16,413
and the action has to land in the short stretch where the selector jack lines

53
00:04:16,433 --> 00:04:18,531
up with the selector.

54
00:04:18,931 --> 00:04:22,141
The selector lags 0.6 milliseconds,

55
00:04:22,161 --> 00:04:28,969
during which the carriage moves another 0.72 millimetres, 0.34 of a pitch,

56
00:04:28,989 --> 00:04:31,461
more than the 0.25 allowed.

57
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:33,921
So the command must go out early.

58
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:38,581
Timing is set by position, not by the clock.

59
00:04:40,101 --> 00:04:42,923
How do we fire at the right place?

60
00:04:43,323 --> 00:04:50,111
The controller always knows carriage position x from the motor encoder or a

61
00:04:50,131 --> 00:04:51,291
linear scale.

62
00:04:51,691 --> 00:04:55,022
Needle i is at x0 plus i times p;

63
00:04:55,042 --> 00:04:59,897
the selection point is offset c on the carriage,

64
00:04:59,917 --> 00:05:03,959
so the ideal action point is xi minus c;

65
00:05:03,979 --> 00:05:10,052
subtract the distance covered during the delay, v times T_d,

66
00:05:10,072 --> 00:05:12,591
and that's where to fire.

67
00:05:12,991 --> 00:05:18,194
Before each course the selection data is loaded into the FPGA,

68
00:05:18,214 --> 00:05:23,839
which compares on every encoder count and fires when it gets there,

69
00:05:23,859 --> 00:05:25,524
within microseconds.

70
00:05:25,924 --> 00:05:30,180
Software only prepares the data once per course.

71
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:36,440
This is the knitting simulation, slowed five times.

72
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:40,865
The upper row is the back bed, the lower row the front bed;

73
00:05:40,885 --> 00:05:45,389
the grey zones are the reversal margins, where the carriage slows,

74
00:05:45,409 --> 00:05:47,172
turns and speeds up again.

75
00:05:47,572 --> 00:05:55,832
A red dot marks a needle whose selection position error is out of tolerance.

76
00:05:56,232 --> 00:06:02,581
On the left, no lead: as soon as the carriage gets up to speed,

77
00:06:02,601 --> 00:06:05,310
red dots appear in patches.

78
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:09,022
On the right, lead by real-time speed:

79
00:06:09,042 --> 00:06:14,370
selection is on target whether accelerating or at full speed.

80
00:06:15,890 --> 00:06:21,270
The key is that the v in the lead must be the real-time speed.

81
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:23,983
The carriage reverses every pass,

82
00:06:24,003 --> 00:06:27,730
and after each reversal there's an acceleration zone.

83
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:35,054
With no lead it lags 0.34 of a pitch at full speed, beyond the 0.25 allowed,

84
00:06:35,074 --> 00:06:38,069
so the machine has to run slowly.

85
00:06:38,469 --> 00:06:42,509
A fixed lead tuned for full speed is exact there,

86
00:06:42,529 --> 00:06:47,978
but in the acceleration zone the slower it goes the more it leads,

87
00:06:47,998 --> 00:06:51,209
so right after reversal it fires early.

88
00:06:51,609 --> 00:06:57,837
Only a real-time lead leaves nothing but the uncertainty in T_d itself,

89
00:06:57,857 --> 00:07:02,237
and since T_d drifts with temperature and voltage,

90
00:07:02,257 --> 00:07:05,229
it has to be calibrated regularly.

91
00:07:06,749 --> 00:07:10,049
Two-level control and FPGA selection.

92
00:07:11,569 --> 00:07:15,569
Control is split into two levels with very different time scales.

93
00:07:15,969 --> 00:07:22,140
The main controller works per course: it parses the knitting program and,

94
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:27,737
before each reversal, prepares the next course's direction, speed,

95
00:07:27,757 --> 00:07:33,589
selection data, stitch setting, yarn carriers, racking and take-down.

96
00:07:33,989 --> 00:07:38,449
A course takes hundreds of milliseconds, plenty of time.

97
00:07:38,849 --> 00:07:41,776
The carriage board works per needle:

98
00:07:41,796 --> 00:07:45,869
its FPGA fires the selectors by carriage position,

99
00:07:45,889 --> 00:07:49,307
with only a millisecond or two per needle.

100
00:07:49,707 --> 00:07:55,764
The board rides on the carriage so dozens of coils wire up locally and only

101
00:07:55,784 --> 00:07:59,411
power and one bus go through the cable chain.

102
00:07:59,811 --> 00:08:03,322
The price is vibration and acceleration;

103
00:08:03,342 --> 00:08:09,679
typical faults are loose connectors and broken cores in the chain cable.

104
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:15,671
Worked example: time per front panel.

105
00:08:17,191 --> 00:08:20,459
Now tie it together to answer the opening question.

106
00:08:20,859 --> 00:08:24,184
Step one: can the selector keep up?

107
00:08:24,584 --> 00:08:28,701
Each needle gets 1.76 milliseconds,

108
00:08:28,721 --> 00:08:37,448
more than the selector's 1.2-millisecond engage–release cycle T_c, so yes.

109
00:08:37,848 --> 00:08:43,602
Step two: a fixed lead needs full speed before the first needle,

110
00:08:43,622 --> 00:08:49,014
so the margin is at least v squared over 2a, 72 millimetres,

111
00:08:49,034 --> 00:08:52,803
and one pass is the 582-millimetre fabric,

112
00:08:52,823 --> 00:08:59,748
plus the 150-millimetre span of the cam plate, plus two 72s: 876 millimetres.

113
00:09:00,148 --> 00:09:09,733
Time is D over v, plus v over a, plus a 0.05-second dwell at reversal:

114
00:09:09,753 --> 00:09:16,867
about 0.90 seconds, so 760 passes take 11.4 minutes.

115
00:09:18,387 --> 00:09:25,005
On a robot sorting line, the conveyor motor's encoder gives belt position;

116
00:09:25,025 --> 00:09:30,387
the camera must fire when a part reaches the centre of view,

117
00:09:30,407 --> 00:09:37,474
and the dispensing robot must open its valve when the part reaches a set point.

118
00:09:37,874 --> 00:09:43,191
It's the same problem as needle selection: timing by position,

119
00:09:43,211 --> 00:09:44,654
not by the clock.

120
00:09:45,054 --> 00:09:47,727
The engineering is the same too:

121
00:09:47,747 --> 00:09:54,796
load the target positions into the position-compare output of a motion card or FPGA,

122
00:09:54,816 --> 00:10:01,613
which checks every encoder count in hardware and fires a trigger when it arrives.

123
00:10:02,012 --> 00:10:05,769
Cameras and valves have delays as well,

124
00:10:05,789 --> 00:10:12,353
so when belt speed varies they too must lead by the real-time speed.

125
00:10:13,872 --> 00:10:20,772
In everyday life the closest thing to a flat knitting machine is an inkjet printer.

126
00:10:21,172 --> 00:10:24,980
The print head sweeps back and forth across the paper,

127
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,822
printing a band each pass,

128
00:10:26,842 --> 00:10:32,422
and every nozzle decides to fire or not when it reaches exactly the right spot,

129
00:10:32,442 --> 00:10:37,738
almost exactly like the carriage sweeping the beds and each needle deciding

130
00:10:37,758 --> 00:10:38,872
whether to knit.

131
00:10:39,272 --> 00:10:45,024
The head's position comes from an encoder strip, and it fires by position,

132
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:45,960
not by time,

133
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:51,967
which is why the text isn't skewed even while the head is accelerating at the

134
00:10:51,987 --> 00:10:53,215
start of a pass.

135
00:10:54,735 --> 00:10:57,275
Back to the opening question.

136
00:10:57,675 --> 00:11:05,658
Now each pass takes 0.90 seconds and a panel 11.4 minutes.

137
00:11:06,058 --> 00:11:12,860
Option A, a real-time lead, needn't reach full speed before the first needle,

138
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:17,467
so the margin drops to 20 millimetres: 10.3 minutes.

139
00:11:17,867 --> 00:11:21,746
Option C, 1.6 metres per second,

140
00:11:21,766 --> 00:11:28,204
needs a 128-millimetre margin and gives 10.5 minutes.

141
00:11:28,604 --> 00:11:32,645
A third more speed buys only about 8 percent,

142
00:11:32,665 --> 00:11:37,699
because v over a and v squared over 2a both grow with v,

143
00:11:37,719 --> 00:11:42,572
and the time per needle approaches the selector cycle.

144
00:11:42,972 --> 00:11:49,758
Option B, a two-system carriage, knits both colours in one pass,

145
00:11:49,778 --> 00:11:55,819
460 passes instead of 760: 7.9 minutes, the biggest gain,

146
00:11:55,839 --> 00:12:00,072
because it reduces the number of passes.

147
00:12:00,472 --> 00:12:05,012
Its price is a longer, heavier carriage.

148
00:12:05,412 --> 00:12:11,408
Option A is little more than a software change, so do it first.

149
00:12:12,928 --> 00:12:17,377
Now Virtual lab 17-1, the flat knitting simulation.

150
00:12:17,397 --> 00:12:21,584
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

151
00:12:21,604 --> 00:12:26,666
The results and tasks at the lower right are blurred here,

152
00:12:26,686 --> 00:12:29,558
so you can read them on your own.

153
00:12:29,578 --> 00:12:33,589
Top left are the needle beds and the carriage,

154
00:12:33,609 --> 00:12:38,058
with the red line at the selection point; below it,

155
00:12:38,078 --> 00:12:44,631
the loops as they're knitted; on the right, the pattern and the parameters.

156
00:12:44,651 --> 00:12:51,028
It's knitting a two-colour jacquard with a fixed lead, slowed five times.

157
00:12:51,428 --> 00:12:52,319
Task one:

158
00:12:52,339 --> 00:12:59,507
choose no lead and raise the carriage speed from 0.4 metres per second.

159
00:12:59,527 --> 00:13:06,289
No red dots yet. Keep going; at which step do mis-selections start?

160
00:13:06,309 --> 00:13:12,768
Find it yourself, then explain it with p over v and v times T_d.

161
00:13:13,168 --> 00:13:16,734
Task two: back to a fixed lead at 1.2,

162
00:13:16,754 --> 00:13:23,811
and shrink the reversal margin from 200 millimetres. Where do errors start,

163
00:13:23,831 --> 00:13:29,001
on which side of the fabric and which needles, and why?

164
00:13:29,021 --> 00:13:31,360
That goes in your report.

165
00:13:31,760 --> 00:13:38,276
Task three: switch to the real-time lead and try a few gauges,

166
00:13:38,296 --> 00:13:42,494
comparing how the maximum error changes.

167
00:13:42,514 --> 00:13:48,820
How fast the carriage can go on each gauge is yours to find.

168
00:13:49,220 --> 00:13:56,252
Task four: back to E12, widen the reversal margin from 40 to 150 millimetres,

169
00:13:56,272 --> 00:14:02,663
and compare the time per course and the panel estimate in the results.

170
00:14:02,683 --> 00:14:05,320
How big should the margin be?

171
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:06,449
Finally,

172
00:14:06,469 --> 00:14:13,477
change the selector delay from 0.8 to 0.6 milliseconds and repeat task one.

173
00:14:13,497 --> 00:14:17,694
How does the speed where errors begin change?

