183
00:15:03,412 --> 00:15:06,186
Your assignment has four questions,

184
00:15:06,206 --> 00:15:11,295
all about knitting sleeves on an E14 single-system machine. One:

185
00:15:11,315 --> 00:15:14,488
with the carriage at 1 metre per second,

186
00:15:14,508 --> 00:15:20,635
a 0.5 millisecond selector delay and a 1.3 millisecond cycle, find the pitch,

187
00:15:20,655 --> 00:15:22,232
the time per needle,

188
00:15:22,252 --> 00:15:27,660
the lag without compensation and whether it exceeds a quarter pitch,

189
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:30,773
and the highest carriage speed allowed.

190
00:15:31,173 --> 00:15:36,698
Two: the sleeve is 180 needles and 500 courses with a fixed lead.

191
00:15:36,718 --> 00:15:41,475
Find one pass's travel and time and the time per sleeve,

192
00:15:41,495 --> 00:15:47,788
then estimate how much a real-time lead with a 20 millimetre margin saves.

193
00:15:48,188 --> 00:15:53,729
Three is closed-loop control of loop length, which this video skipped;

194
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:59,449
it's in the slides and the textbook. The yarn measures 1026 millimetres:

195
00:15:59,469 --> 00:16:05,010
deeper or shallower, by how many steps, and why is the gain below one?

196
00:16:05,030 --> 00:16:06,678
Four is a discussion:

197
00:16:06,698 --> 00:16:11,842
what do you gain by knitting two sleeves side by side on one bed,

198
00:16:11,862 --> 00:16:16,688
and what must the program and the yarn carriers take care of?

199
00:16:17,088 --> 00:16:20,790
Submit the report on the WenQuest platform.

200
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:26,480
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

201
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:33,130
a needle is selected at S1 but not at S2. What does it do in this course?

202
00:16:33,530 --> 00:16:38,528
Or, on an E7 machine with the carriage at 1 metre per second,

203
00:16:38,548 --> 00:16:41,654
what is the selection time per needle?

204
00:16:41,674 --> 00:16:45,521
Another asks why take-down uses torque control.

205
00:16:45,921 --> 00:16:51,741
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

206
00:16:53,261 --> 00:16:55,161
Three things to remember.

207
00:16:55,561 --> 00:17:00,437
One: on a flat knitting machine the carriage moves,

208
00:17:00,457 --> 00:17:05,429
its cams are sliding cams that fix how needles move,

209
00:17:05,449 --> 00:17:12,437
and the electronics only makes one or two yes-or-no decisions per needle,

210
00:17:12,457 --> 00:17:13,589
by position.

211
00:17:13,989 --> 00:17:19,538
Two: selection fires by position; each needle gets only p over v,

212
00:17:19,558 --> 00:17:22,794
and the lead must use real-time speed.

213
00:17:23,194 --> 00:17:25,125
Three: two-level control,

214
00:17:25,145 --> 00:17:30,980
with the main controller preparing each course and the FPGA on the carriage

215
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:32,854
firing needle by needle.

